  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA weak Pacific trough is expected to approach from the Desert \nSouthwest towards the end of the weekend, which doesn't appear \nstrong enough at this time to bring a cold front through the region. \nHowever, a weaker cold front may stall to the north or near the. \nCWA.  This trough, despite not being very strong, is also expected \nto effectively break down high pressure over Central Texas and push \nit eastward, creating somewhat unsettled conditions and drawing \nincreased moisture into the region. \n\nBeginning Monday night into Tuesday, rain chances increase for \nCentral Texas to around 20 to 35% as increased Gulf moisture arrives \ninto to the area. Rain chances could trend higher or lower for this \nday depending on the behavior of any cold fronts associated with the \nsystem (if one stalls in the vicinity or just north of the region, \nrain chances could trend a little higher).  Conditions will remain a \nbit unsettled for a few days as a weak secondary shortwave moves \nacross the area behind the main trough. Resultantly, low rain \nchances (20% and less) prevail in the afternoons for Wednesday and \nThursday as well, with the better chance being on Thursday.\n\nTemperatures are expected to remain warm, with highs reaching the \nupper 70s/lower 80s each day.  Lows will be cool each night, getting \ndown into the upper 50s/lower 60s each night through Wednesday \nnight.  Light to moderate overall easterly winds are expected",
